note: |
  ## Triage and Analysis

  ### What triggered this alert
  The crontab command was executed with the edit flag by a
  non-privileged user on a Linux host indicating a possible
  attempt to establish persistence via a scheduled cron job.

  ### Possible investigation steps
  - Identify the user who ran crontab and determine whether
    they have a legitimate business reason to modify cron jobs
  - Check the current crontab entries for the user by reviewing
    file write events to /var/spool/cron/ around the same time
  - Examine the parent process that spawned crontab to determine
    whether it was an interactive shell or an automated process
  - Correlate with other suspicious activity from the same user
    such as downloads, reverse shells, or privilege escalation
  - Check /etc/cron.d/ /etc/cron.daily/ and /etc/cron.hourly/
    for any newly created or modified files in the same window

  ### False positive analysis
  - Developers or administrators legitimately scheduling backup
    log rotation or maintenance scripts via personal crontabs
  - Automated configuration management tools such as Ansible
    or Puppet that manage cron entries as part of provisioning

  ### Response and remediation
  - Contain: review and remove any malicious cron entries
    immediately to prevent scheduled execution of payloads
  - Collect: capture the full crontab content for the user
    preserve any scripts referenced in the cron entries
  - Eradicate: delete malicious cron entries remove any
    payloads or scripts scheduled to run by the attacker
  - Recover: audit all crontabs across the host for other
    malicious entries rotate credentials for affected accounts
  - Escalate immediately if the cron entry references a
    network callback curl wget or reverse shell command

  ### References
  - https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1053/003/

false_positives:
  - "Developers or administrators scheduling legitimate maintenance scripts via crontab"
  - "Configuration management tools managing cron entries during provisioning"

references:
